9.2  Journaling: how a filesystem survives a crash

TL;DR
  - Creating a file touches several places on disk: the inode, the folder, the
    free-space map. A crash in between would leave them disagreeing.
  - A journal writes the whole change to a log area first, then applies it.
    After a crash, the filesystem replays or drops the logged changes:
    consistent again in seconds, without a full check.
  - ext4 and XFS journal metadata; your file's content still needs fsync (4.5).

      findmnt -no OPTIONS /        # mount options of the root filesystem
      sudo dmesg | grep -iE 'ext4|xfs|btrfs'   # errors and journal replays

On your machine
  /         btrfs on /dev/nvme0n1p2 · SSD mode
  /home     btrfs on /dev/nvme0n1p2 · SSD mode
  /var/lib/my data btrfs on /dev/nvme0n1p2 · SSD mode
  /boot/efi vfat on /dev/nvme0n1p1
  /media/usb exfat on /dev/sda
  /home     btrfs on /dev/nvme0n1p2

Pitfalls
  - "Read-only file system" out of the blue: the kernel found an error and
    remounted it read-only (errors=remount-ro). Look at dmesg, then plan a
    check: the disk may be failing.
  - Never run fsck on a mounted filesystem: it can destroy it. Check from a
    rescue system, or let it run at boot.
  - fsck -n only reports problems; without it, fsck changes the disk.

Deep dive

  ▾ What does ext4's default mode, data=ordered, promise?

    Metadata goes through the journal; file data isn't journaled, but it's
    written before the metadata that points to it is committed. So after a
    crash, a file may hold its old content or its new content, never blocks of
    someone else's deleted data. data=journal also journals the data: safer, and
    much slower.

  ▾ Why does a journal make the check fast?

    Without one, the only way to know the filesystem is consistent after a crash
    is to read every inode and folder: minutes to hours on a large disk. With a
    journal, only the last few seconds of changes can be unfinished, and they're
    all in one small area.

